# coverage38: can worst-case coverage beat c = 3/8? New designs, reciprocity, dichotomy, hypotheses

Agent `coverage38`, 2026-10-18 (phase 6).
- Pre-registration: `notes/coverage38_prereg.md` (commit 757af72, before any registered run).
- Code: `src/coverage38/{theory,lehman,oracle,experiments,analyze}.py`. Tests: `tests/test_coverage38.py`.
- Raw data: `experiments/raw/coverage38_{e2,e2s,e3}.jsonl`; verdicts:
  `experiments/analysis/coverage38_summary.json`.
- GPT-6.1-sol: blind round `codex/coverage38_1.out`; adversarial review `codex/coverage38_2.out`.
  Opus critic: §9.
- Notation is that of notes/dchworst_results.md (Theorems W, R; barriers B1–B4).

## 0. Verdict (plain language)

**No worst-case exponent below 1/5 is proved, and no log-factor gain either. The barrier is
now sharper and design-independent.** Three proved statements, all in the project's cost
model, explain why.

- **Theorem U (proved; GPT-6.1-sol derived the same inequality blind).** In the DCL/Theorem-R
  design space, the cost is governed by two numbers only: J, the number of distinct moduli,
  and λ, the expected number of covering events for a random pair. For **every** design
  (any moduli, any scales, prime or composite, prime powers, any weight set, full or Lehman
  stationary-point windows of any width):

    cost ≥ c · N^{1/5} · (λ⁴/J)^{1/5}.

  A single dyadic band attains this. So *every* design needs the same coverage statement to
  beat 1/5: **all pairs covered at λ < J^{1/4}**. That is Theorem W's c > 3/8, now shown to
  be design-invariant. No design family is weaker. Wide ranges, multiscale bands and
  composite moduli are never better than one band, and composite moduli are worse in the
  hitting probability (Ford's divisor clustering).
  - If coverage is provable at λ = J^θ, the exponent is (1+θ)/(6+θ). This is 1/6 at θ = 0
    and 1/5 at θ = 1/4.
  - A log gain needs the same: λ ≤ J^{1/4}(log N)^{−a} buys (log N)^{4a/5}.
- **Proposition L, the linear-statistics barrier (proved).** Put J points uniformly at random
  in the complement of the target set, so that they have zero hits. With probability ≥ 1/4,
  every one of Q prescribed bounded linear statistics stays within 2λ + 2√(J log 8Q) of its
  uniform-model value. Exponential sums, Kloosterman sums and character sums are all such
  statistics.
  - So no pointwise bound at the generic √J level certifies coverage below λ ≍ √J. Known
    deduction routes (Erdős–Turán, smoothing) need λ ≳ √J even with square-root
    cancellation.
  - Since Theorem U needs λ < J^{1/4}, **pointwise exponential-sum information cannot give
    any gain in any design.** That includes Hooley's R*, GRH-strength character bounds,
    Karatsuba, Bourgain–Garaev and Korolev.
  - The literature has no nontrivial bound for prime-variable Kloosterman sums at our length
    q^{0.32}. Korolev and Bourgain show nontrivial bounds only from q^{1/2+ε}. For integer
    variables only log savings exist, Y·log log q/log q (Korolev 2016).
- **What would suffice (conjectured, not standard).** A Poisson-precision count of band moduli
  whose reciprocal points p·ℓ̄ mod q fall in the Farey-arc set. Equivalently, one of H
  dilates Bp of a residue class mod q must be a product (band prime) × (integer ≤ Hq/Y).
  The product size is ≈ q^{1+1/25}. Unconditional "every class is a small product" results
  need size q^{3/2}. R* gives positivity only at λ ≳ √J. I found no standard named
  hypothesis implying it. The checks covered GRH, Elliott–Halberstam, R*, Montgomery,
  Cramér-type, Erdős–Odlyzko–Sárközy and equidistribution of t_ℓ.
- **Dichotomy (proved in part, refuted as a source of gain at the sizes tested).**
  - Every linear family (p, q) = (ax+b, cx+d), including (x, x+1), has a constant
    δ = Bp − Aq at a fixed weight. Lehman/Fermat factors such N in polylog time. **So barrier
    B3 does not constrain "covered or Lehman-easy" proofs.**
  - B3 survives in a sparse non-interval form. With α badly approximable, pairs
    (x, ⌊αx⌋) are Dirichlet-tight, and coverage for them gives a sparse covering statement
    for {Bx − A⌊αx⌋} (GPT-6.1-sol).
  - The data say the two failure sets are **not** disjoint. Removing every Lehman-easy pair
    changes the exhaustive worst case over S_32 and S_36 by **0** in all 14 cells. At P ≈ Y³
    (n = 60, 72) the uncovered rate of Lehman-easy pairs is 0.97–1.01 times that of the hard
    pairs (|z| ≤ 0.55). All pre-registered predictions D1–D4 and S1–S2 hold.
- **Public intervals.** Coverage of the true pair is a statement about the hidden integers
  δ_v, not about a public interval.
  - The only N-public interval statement that implies it is full-candidate covering: every x
    in [√(N/2), √N] has some Bx² − AN with a band divisor.
  - That is a Jacobsthal-type covering by N-determined quadratic classes. No theorem of the
    Jutila / Friedlander–Lagarias / Harman / Baker–Harman / Ford type applies to it.
- **e3: the barrier is realised by real arithmetic.** See §7.3 for the numbers.
- **Conditional results.** None from standard number-theoretic hypotheses. Two remarks:
  - Under the complexity hypothesis P = BPP, padding plus Lenstra–Pomerance already gives
    deterministic subexponential factoring (GPT-6.1-sol). That is not a coverage statement.
  - The known conditional deterministic results remain Schoof 1982 (N^{1/5+ε}, GRH) and
    Umans–Wang (1/6, SPDC). We found no deterministic sub-1/5 result under a standard
    number-theoretic hypothesis.

## 1. Setting

- N = pq, P = √N, (p, q) ∈ S_n (p < q ≤ 2p primes).
- A weight is a primitive v = (A, B), B ≥ 1, A ≠ 0, of height h(v) = max(|A|, B). Write
  δ_v = Bp − Aq.
- A design D(N) is a finite set of targets. A target has a modulus M, a weight v, a root of
  Br² ≡ AN (mod M) and a window of g-values; DCL's class lock is mod M² (HH 2022 Prop 3.3).
  Group the targets by (v, M).
- π_{v,M} is the probability, over uniform (p, q) ∈ S_n, that some target in group (v, M)
  covers the true pair.
- λ = Σ π_{v,M}. J is the number of distinct moduli. T is the number of groups.
- **Cost model (𝔐⁺, below16 §1).** X ≥ max(T, (Σ_τ |W_τ|)^{1/2}). Theorem R realises
  X = Õ(T + (Σ|W_τ|)^{1/2}) + N^{1/6+o(1)} by choosing the baby length m, so the model is
  tight for this family.
- **Window hypothesis (WIN).** Σ_{τ∈(v,M)} |W_τ| ≥ c₁ · π_{v,M}² · h(v) · P.
  - *Full windows* (D′, Theorem W): π ≍ 1/M and |W| ≍ h(v)P/M². The S-range of Bx + AN/x
    over [√(N/2), √N] is ≥ 0.02·h(v)P for every weight, which fixes c₁.
  - *Lehman / stationary-point windows*: only |δ_v| ≤ Δ is searched.
    - S_v − 2√(ABN) = δ_v²/(S_v + 2√(ABN)), so the window has ≍ Δ²/(h(v)PM²) g-values.
    - The coverage probability is π ≍ Δ/(M h(v) P).
    - Both relations give WIN with equality up to constants.
  - *Arbitrary window placements*: the offset u = S_v − 2√(ABN) has density ∝ u^{−1/2}, which
    is largest at the stationary point. So an interval of the same size placed anywhere
    covers no more. This general case is a model statement (UNVERIFIED as a theorem about
    prime pairs).

## 2. Theorem U (universal cost bound) — proved in the model

**Theorem U.** Under 𝔐⁺ and WIN, every design satisfies

  X ≥ (c₁P/(2√3))^{2/5} · λ^{4/5} · J^{−1/5},  and also  X ≥ (c₁P/(2√3))^{1/3} · λ^{2/3}.

*Proof.*
- Fix a modulus M, carrying t_M groups with distinct weights v, and put u_M = Σ_v π_{v,M}.
- Cauchy–Schwarz gives

    Σ_v π_v² h(v) ≥ u_M² / Σ_v h(v)^{−1}.

- **Height count.** At most 3h² primitive weights have height ≤ h. So the k-th smallest
  height is ≥ √(k/3), and Σ_v 1/h(v) ≤ √3 Σ_{k≤t_M} k^{−1/2} ≤ 2√3 √t_M.
  (`test_kth_height_bound` checks this.)
- Hence Σ_τ |W_τ| ≥ (c₁P/(2√3)) Σ_M u_M² t_M^{−1/2}.
- Hölder with exponents (2, 4, 4):

    λ = Σ_M (u_M² t_M^{−1/2})^{1/2} · t_M^{1/4} · 1 ≤ (Σ_M u_M² t_M^{−1/2})^{1/2} T^{1/4} J^{1/4}.

- So Σ|W| ≥ (c₁P/(2√3)) λ²/(TJ)^{1/2}.
- With X ≥ T and X ≥ (Σ|W|)^{1/2} this gives X^{5/4} ≥ (c₁P/(2√3))^{1/2} λ J^{−1/4}.
- The second bound follows from J ≤ T ≤ X. ∎

The moduli sizes cancel out entirely. Nothing about bands, primality or scale enters.

**Attainment (proved by direct optimisation; `test_single_band_optima_match_universal_formula`).**
- **One band, full windows.** T = λY, Σ|W| = λHP/Y and H² = λY/J. The optimum is at
  Y = (P²/(λJ))^{1/5}, where X = P^{2/5}λ^{4/5}J^{−1/5}. This is feasible while
  J ≤ (P²/λ)^{1/6}.
- **One band, Lehman windows.** T = JH² and Σ|W| = λ²P/(JH), with Y free in [J, JH²/λ]. The
  optimum is H = (λ√P)^{2/5}J^{−3/5}, with the same X.
- Equality in Hölder forces equal t_M and equal u_M across moduli. With full windows
  t_M = u_M M, so it also forces equal M: **a single dyadic band is the extremal design.**

**Corollaries (proved in the model).**
- **U1 (the required statement).** A design that covers every pair at cost X = N^{1/5−η} has
  λ ≤ C·J^{1/4}·N^{−5η/4}. So any worst-case proof through Theorem R must establish coverage
  for a design whose expected count is below the fourth root of its number of moduli. For
  J ≍ Y that is Theorem W's c > 3/8 (H ≤ Y^{1/8−δ}, P ≍ Y^{25/8}). This is the weakest
  coverage statement over all designs of the family.
- **U2 (θ-exponent).** Suppose coverage is provable at λ = J^θ.
  - A single band then gives N^{(1+θ)/(6+θ)}: 1/6, 9/49, 1/5 and 3/13 at θ = 0, 1/8, 1/4 and
    1/2.
  - For θ ≥ 1/4, Theorem U gives X ≥ cN^{1/5}J^{(4θ−1)/5} ≥ cN^{1/5} for every design. The
    unconditional fallback (J = 1, Minkowski) is exactly 1/5.
- **U3 (the 1/6 floor).** For λ ≍ 1, X ≥ cN^{1/6}.
  - This is the average-case floor of multimod Thm A and below16 T1, recovered in this model
    by a two-line argument.
  - It also means no design covering a constant fraction of pairs beats N^{1/6}.
- **U4 (log factors).** λ = J^{1/4}(log N)^{−a} buys N^{1/5}(log N)^{−4a/5} in the list cost.
  - Theorem R's own polylog overhead must be tracked against Harvey–Hittmeir's
    N^{1/5}log^{16/5}N/(log log N)^{3/5}.
  - No such coverage statement is proved. A log gain needs the same J^{1/4} beating of the
    trivial union bound.

**Design families (orchestrator item 1).** Each row is a corollary of Theorem U. Ford's
divisor clustering is the only extra input.

| design | Theorem W analogue | verdict |
|---|---|---|
| one dyadic prime band, full windows | λ⁴ < J (c > 3/8) | extremal |
| Lehman / stationary-point windows, any Δ (span ∝ m² near the stationary point) | λ_Δ⁴ < J, with λ_Δ counting only the \|δ_v\| ≤ Δ events | same threshold. Using the full-window λ for Lehman designs is wrong (GPT-6.1-sol) |
| wide range (Y, Y^{1+κ}] | λ ≍ H² log(1+κ); bound unchanged | never better than one band (Hölder equality fails) |
| multiscale bands with per-scale weights | additive λ, J | never better; the cost is concave in λ, so splitting λ across scales loses |
| composite / smooth moduli | same inequality, roots ≤ 2^{ω(M)} absorbed in WIN | no better in λ. Worse in Pr[covered]: an integer has a divisor in (Y, 2Y] with probability ≍ (log Y)^{−0.086}(log log Y)^{−3/2} (Ford 2008), while the expected count is log 2, so composite events cluster |
| prime powers ℓ^k | J ≤ #primes near Y^{1/k} | worse (fewer moduli for a given scale) |
| signed weights, (1,1), both factors, rectangles | constants in λ; a square box minimises the window for a given count | same threshold |

**A note on the class lock for composite moduli (proved; also GPT-6.1-sol).**
- D′ Lemma 1 uses only invertibility. Write p = r + Mx with Br² ≡ AN (mod M) and
  gcd(M, ABN) = 1. Then Bp + Aq ≡ Br + ANr^{−1} (mod M²).
- Theorem R's Lemmas O, G and X never use primality of the moduli; they use u_M^{M²} = γ.
- So Theorem R extends to squarefree moduli whose prime factors are ≡ 3 (mod 4) and > H,
  with deterministic roots by CRT.
- Consequence: **a prime-detecting (Harman) sieve is not needed at all**, since integer
  moduli are admissible. Harman's sieve only reduces the prime-moduli count to Type I/II
  counts for integer moduli at the same precision o(λ). The needed precision, not the
  primality, is the obstacle.

## 3. Reciprocity and the linear-statistics barrier

### 3.1 Reciprocal form (proved)

- Suppose ℓ | Bp − Aq, say Bp − Aq = ℓk. Then k ≡ Bp ℓ̄ (mod q), and the exact condition is
  ‖Bpℓ̄/q − Bp/(qℓ)‖ ≤ H/ℓ. The centre moves with (B, ℓ); this correction is GPT-6.1-sol's.
- **Sufficient zero-centred test.** Suppose B ≤ H/4, ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] and
  |Bpℓ̄ mods q| ≤ qH/(4Y). Then the corresponding A has |A| ≤ 3H/4, so the pair is covered at
  height H.
- So for a fixed pair, coverage follows when some band point x_ℓ = p ℓ̄ mod q lies in the
  **Farey-arc set**

    S = {x : ∃ B ≤ H′, |Bx mods q| ≤ k₀},

  which has measure μ ≍ H′k₀/q and expected hits λ = Jμ.
- `test_reciprocal_hit_is_coverage` checks the implication exactly.
- Equivalently, some dilate Bp (B ≤ H′) is congruent mod q to ℓ·k with |k| ≤ k₀. That is a
  product of a band prime and an integer of size ≤ Hq/Y. At the threshold the product size
  is ≈ q^{1+1/25}.

### 3.2 Proposition L (proved)

**Setting.**
- Let q ≥ 2 and J² ≤ q/4.
- For j = 1..J let S_j ⊂ Z/q have |S_j| ≤ q/2, and put λ = Σ_j |S_j|/q.
- Let 𝓕 be a family of Q statistics F(x) = Σ_j f_{F,j}(x_j) with |f_{F,j}| ≤ 1. These are
  bounded linear statistics. Examples:
  - exponential sums Σ_j a_j e(h x_j/q) with |a_j| ≤ 1;
  - character sums;
  - sums over sub-progressions of moduli.

**Claim.** There are pairwise distinct x_j ∉ S_j such that, for every F ∈ 𝓕,

  |F(x) − E_unif F| ≤ 2λ + 2√(J log 8Q).

*Proof.*
- Draw x_j independently and uniformly from C_j = Z/q ∖ S_j.
- **Mean shift.** E_unif f = (1−μ_j)E_{C_j} f + μ_j E_{S_j} f. So
  |E_{C_j} f − E_unif f| = μ_j |E_{C_j} f − E_{S_j} f| ≤ 2μ_j, and summed over j the shift
  is ≤ 2λ.
- **Deviation.** Apply Hoeffding to the real and imaginary parts, whose terms lie in
  [−1, 1]: P(|F − E F| ≥ s) ≤ 4e^{−s²/(4J)}. A union bound over 𝓕 at s = 2√(J log 8Q) has
  failure probability ≤ 1/2.
- **Distinctness.** A collision has probability ≤ J²/(q/2)·(1/2) ≤ 1/4.
- So all three hold with probability ≥ 1/4. ∎

**Complements (proved).**
- **(a) Parseval.** For J distinct points, Σ_{h≠0}|K(h)|² = J(q − J). So
  max_{h≠0}|K(h)| ≥ √(J(q−J)/(q−1)), and no uniform pointwise bound is below ≈ √J.
- **(b) Erdős–Turán sufficiency.** Count = λ + (1/q)Σ_{h≠0} Ŝ(−h)K(h). So pointwise bounds
  |K| ≤ κ certify coverage once λ > κ‖Ŝ‖₁/q, where ‖Ŝ‖₁/q ≍ log H′ for Farey-arc sets.
  Applied per B separately the budget is worse, ≍ Hκ (GPT-6.1-sol).
- **(c) What a valid proof must contain.** A proof that uses only estimates on a family 𝓕 of
  bounded linear statistics needs at least one estimate sharper than 2λ + 2√(J log 8Q).
  Otherwise it would also certify the zero-hit configuration of Prop L.
  - At λ < J^{1/4} that means beating the generic random fluctuation of the statistic.
  - Exponential sums have Var = 1 per term. By (a) they cannot be uniformly below √J.
  - By Bernstein's inequality, a statistic f_j = 1_{T_j} (T_j ⊇ S_j, measure ν) helps only
    if ν ≲ λ²/(J log Q) ≈ J^{−1/2}. So the proof must count hits **at Poisson precision in
    sets almost as small as the targets themselves.** That is the original problem: the
    reduction to coarser equidistribution is impossible.

**Consequence (proved implication + barrier).** Combine Theorem U (needs λ < J^{1/4}) with
Prop L and (a)–(c) (pointwise routes need λ ≳ √J up to a √log Q factor). **No argument whose
analytic input is pointwise bounds for exponential or character sums gives a worst-case
exponent below 1/5 in any design of the family.** This is B1 made rigorous and
design-independent. B1 was a heuristic about error budgets in one design.

### 3.3 Literature status of the sums (orchestrator item 2a)

Our sums are K(a) = Σ_{ℓ∈(Y,2Y]} e(aℓ̄/q), q prime ≈ P, at the threshold length Y = q^{8/25}.

| sum | best bound found | applies at q^{0.32}? |
|---|---|---|
| ℓ prime, q prime | Bourgain 2005 / Baker 2012 (Acta Arith. 156, Thm 1): Y^{1−δ⁴/2000} for Y ≥ q^{1/2+ε}; Garaev / Fouvry–Shparlinski: (Y^{15/16} + q^{1/4}Y^{2/3})q^ε, nontrivial for Y > q^{3/4}; Korolev 2019 (Math. Notes 106): length q^{1/2+ε} | **no**: nothing nontrivial below q^{1/2} |
| ℓ prime, averaged over q | Irving (arXiv 1301.6372): from Q^{1/2} or Q^{2/3} | no (needs averaging; one fixed q here) |
| n integer, q prime | Karatsuba's method; Bourgain–Garaev 2014 Thm 16: Y(log log q)³log q/(log Y)^{3/2}; Korolev 2016 (Math. Notes 100) Cor. 1: Y·log log q/log q for q^{1/4+δ} ≤ Y ≤ q^{1/2−δ} | yes, but only a **log** saving |
| conjectural (Hooley R*, intervals) | √Y·q^ε | a pointwise bound: only λ ≳ √J (Prop L) |

- Garaev–Shparlinski (Mathematika 69, 2023; arXiv 2304.07953) flag a gap in a different
  Korolev 2016 Sbornik lemma. That is not the Math. Notes corollary used above.
- The "small products in residue classes" literature is the right comparison for §3.1's
  product form.
  - xy ≡ a (mod p) with x ≤ X, y ≤ Y′ is solvable for XY′ ≫ p^{3/2}log²p (Weil;
    Browning–Haynes; Shparlinski's uniform count, error p^{1/2+o(1)}).
  - Garaev noted that Ford's theorem makes almost all classes *not* products x·y with
    x, y ≤ p^{1/2}(log p)^{0.086−ε}.
  - Our need is product size Hq ≈ q^{1.04}, with a prime factor in a short interval and an
    H-fold union of dilates. This is beyond every known unconditional range. R* gives it only
    at H ≈ √Y for one dilate (Minkowski level), or λ ≳ √J for the union.

### 3.4 The exact estimate that would give c > 3/8 (orchestrator item 2c)

**Hypothesis K(θ) (conjectured; not a standard named hypothesis).** For every prime
q ∈ (P/√2, √2P), every a ≢ 0 (mod q), and every Farey-arc set S of measure μ with
J_Y μ ≥ J_Y^θ:

  #{ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] prime : a ℓ̄ mod q ∈ S} ≥ 1.

- K(θ) for some θ < 1/4 gives worst-case N^{(1+θ)/(6+θ)+o(1)} by Theorems R and U2.
- K(θ) at θ = o(1) (Poisson-type) gives N^{1/6+o(1)}.
- GPT-6.1-sol's equivalent bilinear form: E = Σ_{h≠0} c_h Σ_{B≤B₀} Σ_ℓ e_q(hBpℓ̄) with
  |E| ≪ √λ (log q)^C. Arbitrary-coefficient square-root bilinear bounds would be false.
  The coefficients must be the prescribed Farey-arc Fourier weights.
- By §3.2(c), K(θ) cannot be deduced from coarser equidistribution. It is essentially
  DCH_pair in reciprocal coordinates.

## 4. Dichotomy designs (orchestrator item 3)

**Proposition D (proved).**
- **(a) Lehman side, operational.** Take weights 1 ≤ A ≤ B ≤ h₀ and scan
  S ∈ [⌈2√(ABN)⌉, ⌈2√(ABN)⌉ + X₀] for S² − 4ABN = D². This finds p whenever some such
  weight has δ_v² ≤ 4X₀√(ABN). The reason is S_v − 2√(ABN) = δ_v²/(S_v + 2√(ABN)).
  - The scan costs ≤ h₀²(X₀+1) square tests; a shared BSGS list costs Õ(h₀² + h₀√X₀).
  - `lehman_factor` (sees only N) implements it. `test_lehman_score_implies_success` and
    `test_dirichlet_bound_all_pairs_small` (all of S_24) check it.
- **(b) Dirichlet.** Every pair has L(p,q) = min_v max(h², δ_v²/(4√(ABN))) ≤ C·P^{2/5}, so
  Lehman alone is 1/5 (Lehman 1974; Harvey's exponent). Dirichlet-tight pairs sit exactly at
  the critical scale. With budget C ≤ N^{1/5−ε} the affordable |δ| is a power below P/G
  (GPT-6.1-sol).
- **(c) Linear families are easy.** Take p = ax + b, q = cx + d, g = gcd(a, c), and the
  weight (A, B) = (a/g, c/g). Then δ_v = (cb − ad)/g is constant, so (a) factors N in
  polylog time. In particular (x, x+1), with δ_{(1,1)} = −1, is Fermat-easy.
- **(d) What remains of B3.**
  - B3 obstructs only a *pure* coverage theorem valid for all coprime pairs. A theorem
    "covered **or** Lehman-easy" is not affected. The two have different quantifiers.
  - But B3 has a non-linear survivor (GPT-6.1-sol). For α ∈ (1,2) badly approximable, the
    pairs (x, ⌊αx⌋) satisfy |Bx − A⌊αx⌋| ≥ c_α x/h − O(h). So they are Dirichlet-tight
    throughout the Lehman range.
  - An all-coprime residual theorem therefore implies band-prime coverage of the sparse set
    {Bx − A⌊αx⌋ : |A|, B ≤ H} for every x. That is again ≍ H² values and not an interval.
  - The prime-pair-only version is not obstructed this way.
- **(e) Residual statement.** The needed theorem is "every Lehman-hard balanced pair is
  covered at λ < J^{1/4}". Theorem U is unchanged: dichotomy alters which pairs must be
  covered, not the λ⁴ < J requirement. Prop L is unchanged too: its zero-hit configurations
  are abstract and do not see Dirichlet-tightness.
- **(f) Other partners.** I found no link from uncovered-ness to the structure of p − 1,
  q − 1 or orders. Uncoveredness is a condition on (p q̄ mod ℓ)_ℓ, i.e. on p in ≈ |V|
  N-determined classes per band prime. p − 1 BSGS, Fermat and Hittmeir see unrelated data.
  No provable disjointness was found (UNVERIFIED that none exists).

**Experiments: the failure sets are not disjoint (§7.1–7.2).** The exhaustive worst case is
identical over all pairs and over Lehman-hard pairs. At P ≈ Y³ the uncovered rate does not
depend on Lehman-easiness.
- The "dead weight" effect explains why: weights with 0 < |δ_v| < Y can never be covered.
  Asymptotically there are only O(1) of them, even for Lehman-easy pairs. This matches
  dchworst e3's +5% "one dead weight" excess for near-rational families.

## 5. Public-interval reformulation (orchestrator item 1, last bullet)

**Proposition P (proved, elementary).**
- In every design of the family, the true pair is covered iff p mod M ∈ R_M(N) for some
  modulus M. Here R_M(N) = {x : M | Bx² − AN for a group (v, M) of the design} is computable
  from N. This holds because Bp² − AN = p·δ_v and gcd(p, M) = 1.
- Hence an N-public statement implying coverage for all pairs with this N must be the
  **full-candidate covering**

    𝒞(N): every integer x ∈ [√(N/2), √N] has M | Bx² − AN for some (v, M).

- 𝒞(N) is a complete covering of an interval by |V| N-dependent quadratic classes per
  modulus.
  - Its heuristic failure count is ≈ P e^{−λ}, so it needs λ ≳ log P. That is still
    compatible with polylog cost.
  - The short-interval theorems (Jutila; Friedlander–Lagarias Lemma 2.2; Harman's
    prime-detecting sieves; Baker–Harman; Ford) concern the classes 0 (mod ℓ), i.e.
    multiplicative structure of integers in intervals.
  - The classes here are ±√(AN/B) (mod ℓ), which are not of that form.
  - Sieve theory proves non-emptiness of sifted intervals (Jacobsthal-type, Iwaniec). It
    cannot prove emptiness at λ ≪ |V|.
- The classes become 0 (mod ℓ) only for linear forms with a public shift, e.g. "x − c has a
  band prime factor". Such statements arise only when the hidden value lies in a known
  interval, i.e. the Lehman-easy case of Prop D(c), where coverage is irrelevant.
- **Answer.** No design reduces worst-case coverage to an unconditional short-interval
  theorem. The (x, x+1) route of B3 yields U(x, H) only as an *implied* statement, a
  barrier. It does not yield a usable one.

## 6. Conditional results (orchestrator item 4)

| hypothesis | what it gives here | verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Hooley R* (Σ_{n∈I} e(an̄/q) ≪ \|I\|^{1/2}q^ε; Iwaniec 1982 p. 110) | pointwise κ = √Y q^ε ⇒ coverage at λ ≳ √J. With one dilate, H ≳ √Y | **insufficient** (Theorem U needs λ < J^{1/4}; Prop L shows pointwise information cannot do better) |
| GRH (Dirichlet L) | character sums over primes, bounded linear statistics. The multiplicative route needs product size q^{2}. Korolev's slides: GRH gives nontrivial Kloosterman sums over primes only for X ≥ q^{1+ε} | insufficient |
| Elliott–Halberstam, Bombieri–Vinogradov, Montgomery AP | information on the moduli set (primes), not on the points x_ℓ. A level of distribution beyond the set size H² is meaningless | insufficient |
| Cramér-type gaps for 𝒮_Y = {n : n has a prime factor in (Y, 2Y]} | applies to contiguous intervals. The relevant sets are rank-2 GAPs (or APs of difference q) | needs a non-standard GAP/AP version, which ≈ DCH_pair |
| Erdős–Odlyzko–Sárközy | products of two primes ≤ m in every class. Our box is ℓ ≍ q^{0.32}, k ≲ q^{0.72} | parameter mismatch; insufficient |
| "equidistribution of t mod ℓ" | sufficient only at Poisson precision on sets of measure ≍ J^{−3/4} (§3.2c) | that is K(θ) |
| P = BPP (complexity) | padding + Lenstra–Pomerance ⇒ deterministic L[1/2] factoring | true but unrelated to coverage (GPT-6.1-sol) |

- **Literature check.**
  - Schoof 1982 (GRH) gives a deterministic N^{1/5+ε}.
  - Umans–Wang (arXiv 2511.10851, Thm 5.5) give 1/6 under SPDC(1/3, 1/3), and < 1/5 for
    max(α, β) < 2/5. GPT-6.1-sol notes a quantifier issue: their conjectures are stated for
    infinitely many sizes.
  - Carella 1308.2891 claims 1/6. The project's r13 referee judged its proof incomplete.
  - We found no deterministic sub-1/5 factoring theorem under a standard number-theoretic
    hypothesis, and the route here does not produce one.

