## 8. Candidate card

- **Name.** Universal coverage-cost bound and linear-statistics barrier for DCL worst-case
  designs.
- **Proved (in the cost model 𝔐⁺ + WIN).**
  - Theorem U and Corollaries U1–U4: the threshold λ⁴ < J for every design, attained by one
    band; the exponent (1+θ)/(6+θ); the 1/6 floor.
  - Prop L with complements (a)–(c).
  - Prop D: the Lehman side is operational, linear families are easy, B3 is circumvented for
    dichotomies, and B3's non-linear survivor.
  - Prop P, the public-interval reformulation.
  - Theorem R extends to squarefree moduli.
- **Experimentally supported.**
  - The dichotomy gives no worst-case change (e2) and no rate change at P ≈ Y³ (e2s).
  - Pointwise statistics of real configurations are indistinguishable between covered and
    uncovered pairs, and from zero-hit random surrogates (e3).
- **Refuted for this model.**
  - Any design whose required coverage is weaker than λ < J^{1/4}.
  - Pointwise exponential-sum routes, including R*, to any gain.
  - Lehman dichotomy as a source of gain at the tested sizes.
- **Conjectured.** K(θ) for θ < 1/4. True heuristically (Poisson); not implied by any standard
  hypothesis we found.
- **Open (most promising question).** A Poisson-precision count of inverses of band primes,
  p ℓ̄ mod q, in a Farey-arc set of measure J^{−3/4}, uniform in p and q. This is the
  reciprocal form of DCH_pair at c > 3/8. Equivalently: one of H ≈ q^{1/25} dilates of every
  residue class mod q is a product of a prime in (q^{8/25}, 2q^{8/25}] and an integer
  ≤ q^{18/25}.

