   337	- In every design of the family, the true pair is covered iff p mod M ∈ R_M(N) for some
   338	  modulus M. Here R_M(N) = {x : M | Bx² − AN for a group (v, M) of the design} is computable
   339	  from N. This holds because Bp² − AN = p·δ_v and gcd(p, M) = 1.
   340	- Hence an N-public statement implying coverage for all pairs with this N must be the
   341	  **full-candidate covering**
   342	
   343	    𝒞(N): every integer x ∈ [√(N/2), √N] has M | Bx² − AN for some (v, M).
   344	
   345	- 𝒞(N) is a complete covering of an interval by |V| N-dependent quadratic classes per
   346	  modulus.
   347	  - Its heuristic failure count is ≈ P e^{−λ}, so it needs λ ≳ log P. That is still
   348	    compatible with polylog cost.
   349	  - The short-interval theorems (Jutila; Friedlander–Lagarias Lemma 2.2; Harman's
   350	    prime-detecting sieves; Baker–Harman; Ford) concern the classes 0 (mod ℓ), i.e.
   351	    multiplicative structure of integers in intervals.
   352	  - The classes here are ±√(AN/B) (mod ℓ), which are not of that form.
   353	  - Sieve theory proves non-emptiness of sifted intervals (Jacobsthal-type, Iwaniec). It
   354	    cannot prove emptiness at λ ≪ |V|.
   355	- The classes become 0 (mod ℓ) only for linear forms with a public shift, e.g. "x − c has a
   356	  band prime factor". Such statements arise only when the hidden value lies in a known
   357	  interval, i.e. the Lehman-easy case of Prop D(c), where coverage is irrelevant.
   358	- **Answer.** No design reduces worst-case coverage to an unconditional short-interval
   359	  theorem. The (x, x+1) route of B3 yields U(x, H) only as an *implied* statement, a
   360	  barrier. It does not yield a usable one.
