    58	    the data support. A proof needs a lower-bound sieve at density λ → ∞ over one dyadic
    59	    band, which bounded-level Bonferroni cannot give.
    60	- **Experiments (pre-registered e1–e5, plus e6 for Theorem R).**
    61	  - The exhaustive worst case over all of S_32 and S_36 (6.2M and 78M pairs) follows the
    62	    random (Poisson) extreme: the fitted ĉ matches in all 8 cases (P4).
    63	    - Frozen P1 failed through a mis-stated prediction (the excluded (1,1) weight).
    64	    - Frozen P2 failed in 4 of 125 cells. That is the number the model itself predicts
    65	      (4.47); one cell is a lone outlier with no structure found.
    66	    - Frozen P3 failed almost only on the side of *better-than-random* coverage (120 of 121
    67	      out-of-range cells).
    68	  - The sampled tails at n = 60–84 (e2), the structured families (e3, ≤ 6% deviations), the
    69	    exhaustive D′-design coverage (e4) and the all-blocks search at n = 64 (e5) all pass.
    70	  - e6, Theorem R: **0** failures on covered pairs, exhaustively over S_26 and S_28 and on 36
    71	    adversarial cells. D′ failed on 42 + 105 covered pairs of S_26, S_28 and on 6 adversarial
    72	    covered pairs; DCL-wc factored every one. Details are in §7.
    73	- **DCH repair (orchestrator item 1, verified).**
    74	  - The paper's algorithmic DCH (all N coprime to 6, every integer candidate, A ≠ B) is false
    75	    on squares: for N = t², p = t, Bp² − AN = (B − A)t² has no band divisor.
    76	  - The repaired form is in §6.
    77	
    78	## 1. Setting and notation
    79	
    80	- N = pq with p < q ≤ 2p primes, n bits. S_n is the population of such pairs, as in D′, and
    81	  P = 2^{n/2}.
    82	- A **weight** is a primitive (A, B) with 1 ≤ |A|, B ≤ H.
    83	  - *Positive* weights form D′'s V (A ≥ 1, A ≠ B).
    84	  - *Signed* weights also allow A < 0, and we allow the diagonal (1,1).
    85	  - The height is max(|A|, B).
    86	- A **block** is a set 𝒫 of primes ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4) with H < ℓ < √(N/2). Theorem R needs
    87	  only H < ℓ. The large-sieve statements need ℓ > H² for positive weights and ℓ > 2H² for
    88	  signed weights, so that the classes are distinct.
    89	- The pair (p,q) is **covered** by (𝒫, V) iff ℓ | Bp − Aq for some (A,B) ∈ V and ℓ ∈ 𝒫.
    90	- For ℓ ∤ pq put t_ℓ = p q^{−1} mod ℓ. Then ℓ | Bp − Aq ⇔ A B^{−1} ≡ t_ℓ. So coverage
    91	  depends only on (t_ℓ)_ℓ.
    92	- μ_ℓ(t) is the least height of a weight with A B^{−1} ≡ t (mod ℓ), the first minimum of
    93	  the lattice Λ_ℓ = Z(p,q) + ℓZ² over the weight set.
    94	- h_L(p,q) = min_{ℓ∈L} μ_ℓ(t_ℓ).
    95	
    96	## 2. Theorem W (cost criterion) — proved
    97	
    98	Let J ≤ (1/2 + o(1))Y/ln Y prime moduli ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4) in (Y, 2Y], so that
    99	j := log_Y J ≤ 1 − o(1) and 0 ≤ c ≤ 1/2, cover every pair with weights of height H = √Y·J^{−c}. The DCL
   100	list cost is X = HP/Y² + JH², up to N^{o(1)}. Theorem R below supplies the extraction
   101	unconditionally. With J = Y^j, j ∈ [0, 1], balancing the two terms gives the exponent of N
   102	
   103	  E(j, c) = (1 + j(1−2c)) / (5 + 2j(1−c)).
   104	
   105	∂E/∂j has the sign of (1−2c)·5 − 2(1−c) = 3 − 8c. So the optimum is
   106	
   107	  min(1/5, (1−c)/(7/2−c)),
   108	
   109	which is below 1/5 iff c > 3/8. Values: c = 1/4 and c = 3/8 give 1/5; c = 2/5 gives 6/31;
   110	c = 1/2 gives 1/6. (`theory.worst_case_exponent` and the brute-force check in
   111	`test_cost_exponent_formula`; GPT-6.1-sol dchworst_1 recomputed this independently.)
   112	
   113	*Remarks.*
   114	- X is the list cost at m = W. With m optimised the cost is ≈ √(T′W) + T′. Its minimum over
   115	  y = log_P Y matches E(j, c) whenever E < 1/5 (critic), so the formula is the achievable
   116	  exponent of this design family.
   117	- The floor 1/5 is the j = 0 case, i.e. Minkowski (Corollary R1).
   118	
   119	For J ≍ Y/log Y, the target c > 3/8 means: for every pair, some primitive (A, B) with
   120	|A|, B ≤ Y^{1/8−δ} has Bp − Aq divisible by a prime in (Y, 2Y], where P ≍ Y^{25/8}.
   121	
   122	## 3. Theorem R (worst-case extraction from coverage) — proved
   123	
   124	**Algorithm DCL-wc** (`src/dchworst/wcalg.py`; operational: it sees only N and the public
   125	parameters H, V, blocks 𝒫_1..𝒫_K, T and B0).
   126	
   127	0. If N < 4, output FAIL. If N is even, output 2. If N = s² is a perfect square, output s.
   128	1. **Base selection (Lemma O).** For b = 2, 3, …, B0:
   129	   - check gcd(b, N);
   130	   - compute r_b, the least r ∈ [1, T_scan] with gcd(b^r − 1, N) > 1, where
   131	     s = ⌊√T⌋ + 1 and T_scan = s⌈T/s⌉ = T + O(√T). Use baby-step giant-step by polynomial
   132	     evaluation: f(X) = ∏_{i=1}^{s}(X − b^{−i}), evaluated at X_j = b^{js}. Then scan the first block j whose value has
   133	     gcd > 1.
   134	   - If that gcd is proper, output it. If no r ≤ T_scan exists, **select b** and go to step 2.
   135	     Otherwise (gcd = N) record r_b.
   136	
   137	   If every b ≤ B0 was recorded, put Λ = lcm_b r_b and test gcd(1 + jΛ, N) for
   138	   1 ≤ j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/Λ.
   139	2. For each block k, with L = ∏_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} ℓ², u_ℓ = b^{L/ℓ²} and γ = b^L:
   140	   - **Targets.** Build the D′ targets τ = (A, B, ℓ, r, c, g_lo, W), with roots of
   141	     Br² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) and the class lock mod ℓ². This is D′ §2.2 step 2 verbatim. For A < 0
   142	     the window bounds are Lo = B a₀ + ⌊AN/a₀⌋ and Hi = B b₀ + ⌈AN/b₀⌉, since
   143	     x ↦ Bx + AN/x is increasing.
   144	   - Choose the baby length m as in D′.
   145	   - **γ pre-step (Lemma G).** Over the babies γ^i (0 ≤ i < m), find the least r ∈ [1, m)
   146	     with gcd(γ^r − 1, N) > 1, using a product tree of the γ^r − 1 and a binary descent.
   147	     - If that gcd is proper, output it.
   148	     - If it equals N, put o = r and run the **equal-order split**. E = L·o is a multiple of
   149	       ord_N(b) whose factorisation is known: the band primes, and o by trial division. For
   150	       each prime power r^e ∥ E, compute y_r = b^{E/r^e} (all at once by the all-but-one
   151	       exponent tree), then gcd(y_r^{r^j} − 1, N) for j = 0..e, and output any proper gcd.
   152	       Otherwise D = ∏ r^{v_r}, with v_r the least j where y_r^{r^j} ≡ 1 (mod N); test
   153	       gcd(1 + jD, N) for 1 ≤ j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/D.
   154	   - **Giants.** G_{τ,j} = u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}·γ^{−(g_lo + jm)} for 0 ≤ j < ⌈W_τ/m⌉. Compute
   155	     f(G) = ∏_i(G − γ^i) at all giants by a remainder tree.
   156	     - Output any proper gcd(f(G), N).
   157	     - Collect the **N-giants**, those with gcd = N.
   158	   - **Exploration (Lemma X).** For each N-giant, descend the subproduct tree of f. At a
   159	     node whose value has gcd = N, evaluate both children: output any proper gcd, and
   160	     descend into the children whose gcd is N.
   161	   - **Exact leaf.** When leaf i (the factor X − γ^i) is reached with G ≡ γ^i (mod N), put
   162	     S = c + ℓ²(g_lo + jm + i) and test whether S² − 4ABN = D₀² is a perfect square. If it
   163	     is, output a proper gcd((S ± D₀)/2, N).
   164	3. Output FAIL.
   165	
   166	All outputs are validated (1 < d < N, d | N).
   167	
   168	**Theorem R.** Let N = pq ∈ S_n, and let the parameters satisfy H < ℓ < √(N/2) for every
   169	band prime. Then:
   170	- (i) DCL-wc outputs a proper divisor of N or FAIL;
   171	- (ii) if (p,q) is covered by (𝒫_k, V) for at least one k, DCL-wc outputs a proper
   172	  divisor;
   173	- (iii) its cost is
   174	
   175	  Õ( Σ_k (m_k + T′_k + |V||𝒫_k| + |𝒫_k| log Y) + B0·T^{1/2} + √N/T + √N/Ψ(√(N/2) − 1, B0) )
   176	
   177	  ring operations mod N plus Õ(|V||𝒫_k|) word operations for target construction. Here
   178	  T′_k = Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m_k⌉ is the giant count.
   179	  - The |V||𝒫_k| term charges the rejected (weight, prime) combinations. It is needed for an
   180	    arbitrary V (GPT-6.1-sol: an adversarial V with 9 592 tested weights and 2 targets).
   181	  - For D′'s V and for sign-symmetric signed families it is ≤ H²|𝒫_k|, already part of the
   182	    list cost. With ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4), exactly one of ±AN B^{−1} is a square, so accepted
   183	    targets pay for the tests.
   184	
   185	  With T = ⌈N^{1/3}⌉ and B0 = ⌈n⁴⌉ the last three terms are N^{1/6}·n^{O(1)}, so the cost
   186	  is N^{1/6+o(1)} + Õ(Σ_k(m_k + T′_k + Y)).
   187	
   188	*Proof.*
   189	
   190	**Lemma O (one-sided order dichotomy).**
   191	- Statement. For b with gcd(b, N) = 1, write o_p = ord_p(b) and o_q = ord_q(b). If
   192	  min(o_p, o_q) ≤ T_scan, the scan returns r_b = min(o_p, o_q), possibly r_b ∈ (T, T_scan].
   193	  The gcd at r_b is proper iff o_p ≠ o_q. If no r ≤ T_scan is found, then
   194	  o_p, o_q > T_scan ≥ T.
   195	- Proof of the dichotomy.
   196	  - b^r ≡ 1 (mod p) iff o_p | r, so the least r with gcd(b^r − 1, N) > 1 is
   197	    min(o_p, o_q).
   198	  - Say r = o_q ≤ o_p. If o_p ≠ o_q then o_p > r, so p ∤ b^r − 1 and the gcd is q.
   199	  - If o_p = o_q, the gcd is N.
   200	  - The BSGS scan visits r = js + i in increasing order inside the first block j with a
   201	    nontrivial value, because b^{js+i} − 1 = b^{i}(b^{js} − b^{−i}).
   202	- Proof of the all-recorded case. Suppose every b ≤ B0 is recorded, so
   203	  ord_p(b) = ord_q(b) = r_b for all b ≤ B0.
   204	  - G_p = ⟨2, …, B0⟩ ≤ F_p^× is cyclic of order lcm_b ord_p(b) = Λ. Likewise mod q.
   205	  - G_p contains every B0-smooth integer in [1, p−1], and these are distinct mod p. So
   206	    Λ ≥ Ψ(p−1, B0), and Λ | p − 1.
   207	  - Hence p = 1 + jΛ with j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/Λ (p < √N), and the enumeration finds p.
   208	  - The elementary bound Ψ(x, (log x)^A) ≥ x^{1−1/A}/(log x)^{A−1} follows from counting
   209	    products of k = ⌊log x/(A log log x)⌋ primes ≤ y = (log x)^A: there are
   210	    ≥ (π(y)/k)^k ≥ (log x)^{(A−1)k} of them. With A = 4 and B0 = ⌈n⁴⌉ ≥ (ln p)⁴, this gives
   211	    √N/Λ ≤ P^{1/4}n³.
   212	- The method is the one Harvey–Hittmeir use for large order mod N (arXiv 2601.11131 v2,
   213	  Lemmas 2.1, 2.3 and §3.1, smooth-number subgroup). The one-sided reading "first r
   214	  ⇒ factor unless the two orders are equal" is the only change. ∎
   215	
   216	**Lemma G (γ pre-step).** By the same argument applied to γ over r < m:
   217	- either a proper divisor is output;
   218	- or ord_p(γ) = ord_q(γ) = o < m;
   219	- or **both ord_p(γ) ≥ m and ord_q(γ) ≥ m**.
   220	
   221	In the equal case, γ^o ≡ 1 (mod N) gives o_p(b), o_q(b) | E = L·o.
   222	- For each prime r | E, y_r = b^{E/r^e} has order r^{v_r(o_p)} mod p and r^{v_r(o_q)}
   223	  mod q.
   224	- If v_r(o_p) ≠ v_r(o_q), then at j = min of the two, gcd(y_r^{r^j} − 1, N) is p or q.
   225	- Otherwise o_p = o_q = D, and D is computed. Since b was selected in step 1,
   226	  D = o_p(b) > T. Also D | p − 1, so the enumeration finds p, at cost ≤ √N/T.
   227	- (In the test mode `force_base`, where step 1 is skipped, the enumeration is still complete
   228	  but its cost is not bounded.) ∎
   229	
   230	**The true target.** Let (A, B) ∈ V, ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k with ℓ | Bp − Aq.
   231	- Then a = A N B^{−1} ≡ p² (mod ℓ) is a nonzero square, and p mod ℓ is one of the two
   232	  computed roots r*. This is D′'s coverage criterion; the sign of A plays no role.
   233	- Lemma 1 (class lock): g = (Bp + Aq − c)/ℓ² ∈ Z. Lemma 2 (window): g_lo ≤ g ≤ g_hi, also
   234	  for A < 0 by monotonicity.
   235	- Let j* = ⌊(g − g_lo)/m⌋ and i* = g − g_lo − j*m.
   236	- Mod p: B + AN − (Bp + Aq) = (p−1)(Aq − B), so u_ℓ^{B+AN−c} ≡ u_ℓ^{ℓ²g} = γ^g. Hence
   237	  G* := G_{τ*,j*} ≡ γ^{i*} (mod p) (D′ Lemma 3; no order hypothesis needed).
   238	
   239	**Lemma X (exploration).** Assume both ord_p(γ) ≥ m and ord_q(γ) ≥ m (Lemma G).
   240	- (a) Every giant G has at most one *p-root* i ∈ [0, m) with G ≡ γ^i (mod p), because the
   241	  γ^i are distinct mod p.
   242	  - A node of the subproduct tree with gcd(f_c(G), N) = N contains a p-root.
   243	  - Hence at most one such node per level.
   244	  - The exploration therefore evaluates at most 2⌈log₂ m⌉ node polynomials per N-giant.
   245	- (b) If G* is an N-giant, its exploration outputs a proper divisor.
   246	  - Every node c on the root-to-leaf-i* path satisfies p | f_c(G*), so gcd(f_c(G*), N) ∈
   247	    {p, N}.
   248	  - By induction along the path, either some node yields p (output), or leaf i* is reached
   249	    with G* ≡ γ^{i*} (mod N).
   250	  - In the latter case S = c + ℓ²(g_lo + j*m + i*) = c + ℓ²g = Bp + Aq.
   251	  - Then S² − 4ABN = (Bp − Aq)² is a perfect square, and {(S ± D₀)/2} = {Bp, Aq}.
   252	  - gcd(Bp, N) = p because 1 ≤ B < q, and gcd(|Aq|, N) = q because 1 ≤ |A| < p. ∎
   253	
   254	**Proof of (ii).** Suppose block k covers (p,q).
   255	- Steps 0–1 either output a divisor or select b.
   256	- In block k, Lemma G either outputs a divisor or gives orders ≥ m mod p and q.
   257	- Then G* is computed.
   258	  - If gcd(f(G*), N) = p, it is output.
   259	  - Otherwise gcd = N and Lemma X(b) applies.
   260	- Earlier blocks can only output divisors or continue. Their FAIL branches, the
   261	  enumerations, are complete by Lemmas O and G, so they cannot abort. ∎
   262	
   263	**Proof of (iii).**
   264	- Step 1 costs Õ(B0 T^{1/2}) plus the Λ-enumeration.
   265	- Per block:
   266	  - the exponent tree costs O(|𝒫_k| log Y) mults;
   267	  - targets, giants and the remainder tree cost Õ(m + T′) (D′ §4);
   268	  - Lemma G costs Õ(m): a product tree plus a binary descent;
   269	  - the equal-order split costs O(|𝒫_k| log Y) plus √N/T.
   270	- Exploration, by Lemma X(a), batched level by level, costs Σ_levels Õ(m + 2#N-giants) =
   271	  Õ(m + T′). Multipoint evaluation of each node polynomial is done at its ≤ 2#N-giants
   272	  assigned points.
   273	- ∎ for the fast variant.
   274	
   275	**The implementation is a correctness prototype, not a cost witness** (both reviews). Three
   276	places fall short of the theorem's bound:
   277	- `run_block` computes each u_ℓ = b^{L/ℓ²} separately, Θ(J² log Y) mults instead of the
   278	  all-but-one tree, which D′'s `all_but_one` implements;
   279	- `equal_order_split` computes each b^{E/r^e} separately;
   280	- Lemma G scans r linearly, and the exploration evaluates each node at each giant
   281	  separately, O(m) per N-giant.
   282	
   283	None of these changes an output. The two "FAIL" aborts in `select_base` and
   284	`equal_order_split` are unreachable for N = pq by Lemmas O and G, as noted in the code.
   285	
   286	**Corollaries (proved).**
   287	
   288	- **R1 (Minkowski, unconditional worst case N^{1/5+o(1)}).**
   289	  - Design: signed weights with (1,1), H = ⌊√(2Y)⌋, and a block of 1 prime ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y].
   290	  - If t_ℓ = 1, the weight (1,1) covers. Otherwise pigeonhole on (a, b) ∈ [0, ⌊√ℓ⌋]² gives a
   291	    primitive (A, B) with |A|, B ≤ √ℓ, B ≥ 1 and A ≡ t_ℓB. So every pair is covered.
   292	  - The cost is HP/Y² + O(Y) + N^{1/6+o(1)}, minimised at Y = P^{2/5}: N^{1/5+o(1)}.
   293	  - This recovers **Harvey's proved exponent 1/5** (Harvey 2021, Math. Comp.) inside the
   294	    DCL framework at c = 0; it is not new.
   295	  - McKee–Pinch 1996 §5 is the weighted-congruence precursor, but its balanced method is
   296	    heuristic N^{1/4+ε}, and its deterministic construction is N^{1/3+ε} (GPT-6.1-sol, from
   297	    the scans).
   298	- **R2 (DCH_pair ⇒ worst case 1/6).** Suppose the following, call it DCH_pair(κ). For all
   299	  large n and every (p,q) ∈ S_n, some block of D′'s design with H = ⌈n^κ⌉ covers (p,q),
   300	  with weights from V or with signed weights. Then DCL-wc factors every N ∈ S_n
   301	  deterministically in N^{1/6}·n^{O(1)}·H^{5/3} bit operations, i.e. N^{1/6+o(1)}.
   302	  - DCH_pair is a hypothesis. Theorem N shows it needs κ ≥ 1/2.
   303	- **R3 (D′ sharpened).** With D′'s own design, DCL-wc fails only on pairs uncovered by every
   304	  block. That set is contained in {(p,q): p ∈ U_0(q)}, uncovered in block 0, without any
   305	  condition on ord_q(2), so it is not D′'s E_b, which carries d > T and the clean block.
   306	  - D′ Lemma 9 applied to the fixed block 0 gives an exceptional fraction O(n²/H²).
   307	  - The r16/sieve2d prime-presieve denominator (sieve2d Prop. 4; checked by GPT-6.1-sol)
   308	    gives O(n/H²). That refinement is not mine.
   309	  - E_a (small ord_q(2)) and E_c (FULL) are gone, and so is D′'s restriction ε ≤ 1/60. Only
   310	    Y > H² (the sieve's distinct classes, ε < 1/7) and H < ℓ remain.
